<BaseLayout
  title="Data-Flow Threat Analysis"
  description="Eighteen-step governed AI transaction data flow across Request Admission, AI provider, Result-to-Action Authorization, enterprise system of record, evidence, and BeaconGuard Verify."
>
  <section class="stack-section">
    <h1>Data-Flow Threat Analysis</h1>

    <p>
      This page identifies the trust boundaries, numbered data flows, threat
      conditions, and deterministic enforcement responses associated with a
      BeaconGuard-governed AI transaction. It supplements the
      <a href="/docs/architecture">System Architecture</a> and
      <a href="/docs/threat-model">Threat Model</a> documentation.
    </p>

    <DataFlowThreatDiagram />

    <h2>1. Scope and trust boundary</h2>

    <p>
      BeaconGuard authorizes whether an enterprise application may invoke an AI
      provider (Request Admission Authorization) and whether a declared intended
      enterprise action may proceed (Result-to-Action Authorization). AI-generated
      results remain untrusted proposals. The AI provider and the enterprise
      system of record are separate trust boundaries. BeaconGuard Verify is a
      read-only functional domain. The Control Plane (Govern) is outside the
      per-request path. The customer IdP does not make the BeaconGuard decision.
    </p>

    <p>Out of scope for this page:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Product marketing claims</li>
      <li>Legal compliance certification or legal admissibility</li>
      <li>Replacement of customer systems of record</li>
      <li>Semantic AI-response content authorization</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>2. Eighteen-step data-flow sequence</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Enterprise application creates governed request.</li>
      <li>Identity and tenant context enter BeaconGuard.</li>
      <li>Request is normalized.</li>
      <li>Request admission is evaluated.</li>
      <li>Request decision evidence is recorded.</li>
      <li>Authorized request reaches the AI provider.</li>
      <li>Provider returns an AI-generated result.</li>
      <li>Result is bound to the governed transaction context.</li>
      <li>Intended enterprise action is declared and normalized.</li>
      <li>Result-to-action authorization is evaluated.</li>
      <li>Result decision evidence is recorded.</li>
      <li>Enterprise action is allowed or hard-stopped.</li>
      <li>Canonical evidence enters the verification path.</li>
      <li>Reviewer accesses Decision Explorer.</li>
      <li>Reconstruction traces source evidence.</li>
      <li>Replay verifies the recorded decision.</li>
      <li>Export produces verification material.</li>
      <li>AI-assisted insight remains outside authority.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>
      Steps 6–7 occur only after admission ALLOW. Steps 12 enterprise-effect
      paths occur only after action ALLOW. DENY at steps 4 or 10 hard-stops the
      corresponding next effect.
    </p>

    <h2>3. Trust inputs</h2>

    <p>
      Typical trusted inputs include signed or otherwise verified request
      metadata, workflow identity, user role, source-system trust,
      approved-pathway status, request identifiers, timestamps, freshness
      windows, the Active Verified Release, provider-result binding / correlation
      context, and the declared intended enterprise action.
    </p>

    <p>Missing or invalid required inputs fail closed as DENY.</p>

