    <h2>5. Enforcement behavior</h2>

    <p>Runtime permission results are binary:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>ALLOW</strong> — required controls are satisfied; after evidence requirements are met, the permitted next step may proceed.</li>
      <li><strong>DENY</strong> — hard stop; no provider invocation or no enterprise effect, as applicable.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      When required approval is absent, BeaconGuard returns DENY with a
      deterministic reason indicating that approval is required. An illustrative
      reason style is <code>APPROVAL_REQUIRED</code>. Governance or human review may
      follow from the Verifiable Decision Record; review does not permit
      execution.
    </p>

    <h2>6. Evidence and Verify</h2>

    <p>
      Every deterministic result produces a Verifiable Decision Record that
      preserves decision context, release identity, control result, and outcome.
      Canonical evidence feeds BeaconGuard Verify for Decision Explorer,
      reconstruction, replay, and export. AI-assisted insights remain outside
      authority. Retention and integrity protection depend on the configured
      evidence provider and shared customer deployment responsibilities.
    </p>

    <h2>7. Residual risks</h2>

    <p>BeaconGuard does not remove residual risk from:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Misconfigured identity providers or trust sources</li>
      <li>Incorrect Compliance Playbook authoring</li>
      <li>Compromised signing keys</li>
      <li>Evidence-provider outages or misconfiguration</li>
      <li>Application bypass outside BeaconGuard’s process boundary</li>
      <li>Misuse after an authorized action, outside the declared action contract, or outside the BeaconGuard-controlled path</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      Those risks remain shared with customer operations and are addressed in the
      broader <a href="/docs/threat-model">Threat Model</a>.
    </p>

